     <p>The ⊞ button shows more detail in the same space, drawn at half and then a quarter size; a third press returns to normal, and its tooltip gives the scale. The ✎ button opens drawing tools (points, lines, smooth curves and areas, in a chosen colour and line style); a curve leaves and joins straight stretches without a corner, the small dot in the middle of each segment adds a point there, and with the tools closed, clicking a drawn line gives its elevation profile. Drawings stay in this browser and can be saved or opened as GeoJSON files. The 📏 button measures distances along several segments, with the height difference and gradient between the ends, or the radius of a curve from three or more points clicked along it. Heights are ground or seabed level, not the track on a bridge or in a tunnel. Missing terrain heights leave gaps in an incomplete elevation profile; its statistics use only available samples and connected stretches. Under the scale bar are the coordinates under the cursor and the zoom (the settings can hide them).</p>
     <h3 id="help-names" tabindex="-1">Names and terrain</h3><p>One language setting applies to map, station and line labels. Only names recorded in the source data are shown; nothing is automatically translated or transliterated. When no name is recorded in the chosen language, a related recorded name is used where one exists, such as the other Chinese script or English, and otherwise the local name.</p>
     <p>Station translations are requested for the current view. Snapshot railways retain multilingual OSM tags; the current operating-line tile provider usually supplies only native names. The shared setting cannot recover translations omitted from a source. Search results use the names returned by the search service.</p>
-    <p>Tilting the map enables 3D land and seabed terrain while terrain is switched on; the 3D map button returns to a top-down view. Brown contours show land elevation; blue contours follow the seabed below sea level. Labels use signed metres or feet according to the unit setting. Heavier index contours are labelled. Seabed depth data is much coarser than land elevation. Around the poles on the globe, relief and contours come from NOAA ETOPO 2022 (about 1.85 km resolution).</p>
+    <p>The mountain-shaped terrain button opens a 3D view of the current location, zooming closer when needed so actual terrain heights can be seen. It leaves the current zoom in place when returning to the overhead map. Tilting the map also enables 3D land and seabed relief when terrain is switched on. Brown contours show land elevation; blue contours follow the seabed below sea level. Labels use signed metres or feet according to the unit setting. Heavier index contours are labelled. Seabed depth data is much coarser than land elevation. Around the poles on the globe, relief and contours come from NOAA ETOPO 2022 (about 1.85 km resolution).</p>
     <p>Roads, cycling paths and walking trails remain visible beneath the railway network. Protected areas, military grounds, religious institutions and heritage sites provide context for drawing proposals. Purple boundaries show Indigenous territories where mapped as non-administrative jurisdictions, separately from administrative and conservation boundaries. The map does not establish legal boundaries or permission to build.</p>
     <p>Infrastructure view marks level crossings (dark brown for road crossings, light brown for pedestrian ones), with their mapped equipment; they come from a worldwide OSM table refreshed weekly: <a href="data/level-crossings/manifest.json">snapshot date and counts</a>; <a href="data/level-crossings/crossings.tsv.gz">download the ODbL table</a>. It marks explicitly tagged street-running track with an ochre roadbed. Street running is refreshed weekly from OSM: <a href="data/street-running/manifest.json">snapshot date and query</a>; <a href="data/street-running/street-running.geojson.gz">download the ODbL extract</a>. It is not inferred from every tramway or a nearby road.</p>
     <p>Zoom in on Infrastructure view for platforms and their boarding edges. Platform numbers and edge references appear from zoom 17; from zoom 19 an edge label pairs its reference with the length of the complete mapped boarding edge, in metres or feet. Platform numbers and lengths can take a moment to load. Signals (purple) appear from zoom 13 in Infrastructure and Train control, with references from zoom 16. Mapped train or subway station entrances (teal) appear from zoom 16; click them for mapped details and an OpenStreetMap link. Signal markers identify their location, not their current aspect. Coverage depends on the provider’s mapped data. Value labels hides their text while keeping the mapped shapes and markers.</p>
